Architecture plan for issues #71-#79

Project shape
- Static browser-only camera capture app using plain HTML, CSS, and ES modules.
- Runtime modules live in `js/`; UI shell is `index.html`; styling is `styles.css`.
- Tests use Vitest with browser API stubs under `tests/`.
- Persistence remains browser-local through `localStorage`; no server database is part of
  this feature set.

Module structure and shared contracts
- Add `js/filter.js` as the shared beauty-filter module.
  - Export `filterSpec(beautyLevel)` returning:
    `{ cssFilter, smoothing: { blurPx }, glow: { blurPx, alpha }, params: { brightness, contrast, saturation, warmth } }`.
  - Export `applyToCanvas(ctx, source, w, h, beautyLevel)` for still-image and video-frame rendering.
  - Clamp and normalize levels inside this module so callers can pass raw slider values safely.
  - Level `0` is a strict no-op: `cssFilter === "none"`, no blur/glow, neutral params.
- Extend `js/capture.js`.
  - `capturePicture(videoEl, { beautyLevel })` delegates drawing to `applyToCanvas`.
  - Add `createFilteredStream(sourceStream, getBeautyLevel)` to render video frames through a hidden canvas and merge the original audio track.
  - `recordVideo` accepts an optional filtered stream path without breaking existing callers.
- Extend `js/app.js`.
  - Own slider wiring, restored beauty level, live preview updates, and passing the active level into picture/video capture and storage.
  - Keep UI state updates centralized so slider, preview, capture, record, and gallery stay in sync.
- Extend `js/storage.js`.
  - Bump stored item key to `snapvault:v2:items`.
  - Migrate `snapvault:v1:items` to v2 by adding `beautyLevel: 0`; leave v1 in place for one release.
  - Store the last-used slider level separately as `snapvault:v2:beauty-level`.
- Extend `js/gallery.js` and `styles.css`.
  - Render a high-contrast badge only when `beautyLevel > 0`.
  - Keep level-0 captures visually unchanged.

Issue dependencies
- #71 is the foundation for all filter math and canvas rendering.
- #72 depends on the existing HTML shell and can use #71 for slider semantics, but should not need capture changes.
- #73 depends on #71 and #72.
- #74 depends on #71 and the existing picture capture flow.
- #75 depends on #71 and should integrate carefully with the existing recording contract.
- #76 can land before gallery display but should coordinate with #72 and capture callers.
- #77 depends on #76 so cards can read `beautyLevel`.
- #78 should follow the implementation issues and cover the shared model, preview/capture/recording integrations, storage migration, and gallery badge behavior.
- #79 should land after behavior is stable enough to document and manually verify.

Key design decisions
- Keep the app dependency-free; use Canvas 2D, CSS filters, `captureStream()`, and existing test tooling.
- Put every beauty-level mapping in `js/filter.js`; callers should not duplicate filter constants.
- Treat invalid or missing beauty levels as `0` for backward compatibility and migration safety.
- Preserve existing public contracts where possible by adding optional parameters rather than changing return shapes.
- Use `requestVideoFrameCallback` when available and fall back to `requestAnimationFrame` for filtered video recording.
- Avoid face detection or identity-specific processing; the feature is a deterministic global visual filter.
- Keep storage migration idempotent and non-destructive.
- Tests should focus on deterministic filter output, no-op behavior at level 0, caller integration, migration safety, and badge visibility.
